import{_ as r,C as s,o as t,c as i,ao as o,b as n,w as a,a as c,E as h,ap as l}from"./chunks/framework.D82yFD7r.js";const g="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR96/assets/reuse.BKXggVct.svg",k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Vectors and Matrix–Vector Products","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"bandwidth_bound.md","filePath":"bandwidth_bound.md","lastUpdated":null}'),m={name:"bandwidth_bound.md"};function u(p,e,y,b,x,w){const d=s("Mermaid");return t(),i("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=o('<h1 id="Vectors-and-Matrix–Vector-Products" tabindex="-1">Vectors and Matrix–Vector Products <a class="header-anchor" href="#Vectors-and-Matrix–Vector-Products" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Vectors and Matrix–Vector Products {#Vectors-and-Matrix–Vector-Products}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><a href="./how_it_works">How a Fast BLAS Works</a> explains how matrix multiplication (<code>gemm</code>) is made fast by cutting the matrices into blocks that stay in the cache. Blocking works because <code>gemm</code> uses every number many times. The simpler routines, which work on vectors or on one matrix and one vector, do not, and this page explains what that changes.</p><p>BLAS groups its routines into three <em>levels</em>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Level</th><th style="text-align:right;">Operates on</th><th style="text-align:right;">Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td style="text-align:right;">vectors</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>axpy!</code> (<code>y += a·x</code>), <code>dot</code>, <code>nrm2</code>, <code>scal!</code>, <code>iamax</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2</td><td style="text-align:right;">one matrix, one or two vectors</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemv!</code> (<code>y = A·x</code>), <code>ger!</code>, <code>symv!</code>, <code>trsv!</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td style="text-align:right;">matrices</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemm!</code>, <code>syrk!</code>, <code>trsm!</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="How-many-times-is-each-number-used?" tabindex="-1">How many times is each number used? <a class="header-anchor" href="#How-many-times-is-each-number-used?" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How many times is each number used? {#How-many-times-is-each-number-used?}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The deciding question is how many times the routine needs each number it reads.</p><p><img src="'+g+'" alt=""></p><p>The figure cuts each matrix into 3 × 3 blocks and <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> into three blocks each. Blocks of the same color are multiplied together: a column of <code>A</code> pairs with the matching block of <code>x</code> (or row of <code>B</code>). To compute the yellow block, the blocks with a black outline are multiplied pairwise and added up. The arrows show the row of <code>A</code> and the column of <code>x</code> (or <code>B</code>) meeting at the result.</p><p>In <code>y = A·x</code>, each block of <code>A</code> takes part in exactly one product. Once it has been used it is not needed again, so there is nothing to keep in a cache for later. In <code>C = A·B</code> the same row of <code>A</code> is needed again for every other block in that row of <code>C</code> (the dashed arrow), so each number of <code>A</code> is needed <code>n</code> times and is worth bringing close once and using repeatedly.</p><p>You can count this for each routine. Take <code>Float64</code> (8 bytes per number) and assume the best case, where every distinct number moves between memory and the processor exactly once:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Routine</th><th style="text-align:right;">Arithmetic operations</th><th style="text-align:right;">Bytes moved</th><th style="text-align:right;">Bytes per operation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>dot</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>2n</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>16n</code> (read <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">8</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>axpy!</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>2n</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>24n</code> (read <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>; write <code>y</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">12</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemv!</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>2n²</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>8n²</code> (read <code>A</code>; the vectors are small)</td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemm!</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>2n³</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>24n²</code> (read <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>; write <code>C</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>12/n</code> (0.012 at <code>n = 1000</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first three rows are constants: making the problem bigger never reduces the bytes needed per operation. Only <code>gemm</code> improves with size, which is what blocking exploits. Routines in the first three rows are limited by how quickly numbers can be delivered, not by how quickly they can be added and multiplied. This is called being <em>bandwidth-bound</em>, bandwidth being the number of bytes per second the memory can deliver. For <code>gemv!</code> this holds until the matrix outgrows L3; the end of this page covers what happens beyond that.</p><h2 id="What-the-cache-sizes-still-do" tabindex="-1">What the cache sizes still do <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-the-cache-sizes-still-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the cache sizes still do {#What-the-cache-sizes-still-do}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Blocking cannot reduce the traffic of a bandwidth-bound routine, but the cache sizes still decide how fast the traffic is. A vector that sits in L1 is delivered faster than one in main memory.</p><p>For <code>axpy!</code> the working set is the two vectors, <code>16n</code> bytes. The example machine (a Zen5 processor with 48 KiB L1, 1 MiB L2 and 16 MiB L3, see <a href="./how_it_works">How a Fast BLAS Works</a>) gives three thresholds. If the same two vectors are used repeatedly, as the benchmarks here do, where they fit decides where they are served from:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Vector length <code>n</code></th><th style="text-align:right;">Working set</th><th style="text-align:right;">Served from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">up to 3,072</td><td style="text-align:right;">up to 48 KiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">up to 65,536</td><td style="text-align:right;">up to 1 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">up to 1,048,576</td><td style="text-align:right;">up to 16 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">larger</td><td style="text-align:right;">more than 16 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">main memory</td></tr></tbody></table>',16)),(t(),n(l,null,{default:a(()=>[h(d,{id:"mermaid-226",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20LR%0A%20%20%20%20S%5B%22x%20and%20y%3Cbr%2F%3E16n%20bytes%22%5D%20--%3E%20Q%7B%22fits%20in%20L1%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Q%20--%20yes%20--%3E%20L1%5B%22runs%20at%20L1%20speed%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Q%20--%20no%20--%3E%20Q2%7B%22fits%20in%20L2%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Q2%20--%20yes%20--%3E%20L2%5B%22runs%20at%20L2%20speed%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Q2%20--%20no%20--%3E%20Q3%7B%22fits%20in%20L3%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Q3%20--%20yes%20--%3E%20L3%5B%22runs%20at%20L3%20speed%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Q3%20--%20no%20--%3E%20M%5B%22runs%20at%20main-memory%20speed%3Cbr%2F%3E(the%20slowest%20regime)%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:a(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[c(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=o('<p>So the same routine has several speed regimes as <code>n</code> grows, and a kernel that is best in one regime need not be best in another. The measured per-routine curves are on the <a href="./performance">Benchmarks</a> page, and <a href="./notes">Performance Notes</a> records where PureBLAS sits against OpenBLAS and AOCL for each.</p><h2 id="What-PureBLAS-does-for-these-routines" tabindex="-1">What PureBLAS does for these routines <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-PureBLAS-does-for-these-routines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What PureBLAS does for these routines {#What-PureBLAS-does-for-these-routines}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Because there is nothing to reuse, the work is about moving data well rather than about blocking:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Read in storage order, with full vectors.</strong> Unit-stride data is processed with SIMD loads that consume whole cache lines (see <a href="./simd">SIMD &amp; Hardware Adaptation</a>). Contiguous <code>Float32</code> and <code>Float64</code> data takes this path; strided data and most other element types use a scalar loop.</p></li><li><p><strong>Keep the small operand close.</strong> <code>gemv!</code> with <code>trans=&#39;T&#39;</code> usually walks several columns of <code>A</code> per pass (four, or eight in one variant), so each piece of <code>x</code> loaded into a register is used for several columns before it is dropped.</p></li><li><p><strong>Pick the kernel by regime and by processor.</strong> For <code>axpy!</code> in the main-memory regime, PureBLAS uses a narrower vector kernel on processors that execute 512-bit operations as two 256-bit halves. The choice is derived from that hardware fact, and was measured 17% faster at <code>n = 3 × 10⁶</code> on Zen4 and slower on Zen3. Tuning knobs like this are described in <a href="./tuning">Tuning Constants</a>.</p></li></ul><p>For large matrices, one further effect appears in <code>gemv!</code>, <code>symv!</code> and <code>ger!</code>. Memory is addressed in pages, 4 KiB by default, and the processor keeps a small table of recent address translations (the TLB). A matrix walked by several columns at once, each column a long way from the next, can overflow that table, and then the translation cost rather than the bandwidth sets the speed. On Zen4 at <code>n = 4096</code>, backing the same matrix with 2 MiB pages made <code>symv!</code> 38.6% faster and <code>gemv!</code> about 18% faster. The <a href="./guide">Guide</a> (section &quot;Large matrices: huge pages&quot;) shows how to allocate with <code>PureBLAS.alloc</code> to get this.</p>',5))])}const v=r(m,[["render",u]]);export{k as __pageData,v as default};
